karadoc wrote:
>
> If I can't get my hands on a touchstone, I throw gems at unicorns to
> work out what's valuable and what's not

I think it's important to learn the types of all gray stones because
they have vaulable uses.  For other types of gems it matters far less.

Since every type of hard gem is valuable you only need to know the glass
of each color to know what gems are valuable.  In general I would rather
the unicorn "is not interested in your junk" to know another color of
soft glass. Especially in the case of glass I would rather know from a
unicorn toss than have it definitely identified by a scroll, spell or
touchstone.  Unidentified glass that you happen to be certain is glass
gets a better price in shops.

The most common uses of valuable gems are to get luck from a coaligned
unicorn or to sell in a shop.  For both of those all you really need to
know is the fact that it is valuable not the exact type.  Nonetheless
at one point I built a table of gem types based on hard/soft status
and color. I still use it whenever I encounter gems even though I know
longer think I need to. It was work to generate it so I continue to
use it.

When I find a new type of gem I check if it is hard or soft.  Among the
hard or soft gems only blue and green are ambiguous so I will name
them like "jacinth" or "emerald aquamarine".  Among the soft ones there
is more amibuity so I will name them like "red-sf-br-6" or
"blue-sf-lv-8" using the level I am on at the time and whether I'm in a
branch or a level of the main dungeon.  Several types of gem have a
certain depth in the main dungeon before they can appear the first time.
As I learn the glass colors I re-call the other types with names like
"opal fourite not dilth" if I first encountered it in the main dungeon
above level 28.  When a specific gem type appears in a special level I
also use that information to eliminate ambiguities in my list.  Over
time I end up being certain of the type of a fair number of gem types
doing this just based on hard/soft and level I first encountered them
in the main dungeon. It's like playing Clue but the process is
straightforward.

To me there's a bit of fun in puzzling this out without spending
identification on them and not doing price ID.

> and whenever I read an id
> scroll or sit on a throne, I bring all my gems into the main inventory
> to hopefully get a full id. (I prioritize unided scrolls / potions
> etc, of course.)

Wands, rings and amulets I also prioritize.  And armor I expect to be
magical.  Among gems that I am willing to spend ID on I will first do
the ones that are ambiguous like if I have ones I have called "jet
obsidian" and "obsidian jet" and only add ones that I am certain of if I
have the available inventory.  I'd rather find out a longsword is
"blessed +0" than find out an amethyst is an amethyst.  I don't want to
know a colored glass is worthless if I ever expect to sell items in a
shop.  Even long after thinking it's important to know the identity of
gems in detail.


